      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The recent reference class and base rate are the six January-June 2026 Delaware prints: 40, 49, 41, 43, 43, and 36 percent. Their mean is 42 percent. The latest observation supports a lower level, while alternating monthly changes provide weak evidence for persistent momentum. August 2025 is retained as a separate seasonal risk comparator."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a shrinkage persistence prior that equally weights the latest June print and the January-June reference-class mean. Level adjustment from the 42 percent base rate is -3 percentage points; momentum, one-off, and policy adjustments are each zero because no sustained direction or verified Delaware mechanism warrants an additional adjustment. Successive changes are 9, -8, 2, 0, and -7 percentage points, with mean -0.8. Their sample variance is 194.8/4 = 48.7, so sigma = 6.97854 percentage points. The one-month normal 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 8.93253. Conservatively accumulating innovations over June-to-August gives 8.93253*sqrt(2) = 12.63250, implying rounded final bounds of 26.4 and 51.6 percent around 39.0 percent. Dispersion is estimated from the fetched whole-percent displays."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Mean = (40+49+41+43+43+36)/6 = 42. Point = 0.5*36 + 0.5*42 = 39.0. Sum of squared deviations of monthly changes from -0.8 = 194.8. sigma = sqrt(194.8/4) = 6.97854. 80% interval = 39.0 +/- 1.28*6.97854*sqrt(2) = [26.3675, 51.6325], rounded to [26.4, 51.6]."
      },
